      "writtenOn": "2019-11-20T21:39:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "re CHURN_MODE: yes, CHURN_MODE does not alter the throughput. When enabled, new values are XOR\u0027ed into the FIFO when it fills up. So more interesting when there are relatively long periods of time between TRNG_READ_DATA reads.\n\nre SLICE_MAX_UPPER_LIMIT: Defines the upper end of the sample slicing. So with SLICE_MAX_UPPER_LIMIT\u003d0 and SLICE_MIN_LOWER_LIMIT\u003d0, you are only getting the LSB (one bit) per sample (samples are what fill the FIFO, which is what TRNG_READ_DATA reads from). Previously, you were getting two bits per sample, hence the reduction in throughput. If you were to set SLICE_MAX_UPPER_LIMIT\u003d0xf and take all bits from each sample, entropy would drop significantly. SLICE_MAX_UPPER_LIMIT\u003d1 was found during characterization to pass NIST/DIEHARDER for all samples tested over process, voltage and temperature. Realistically you could probably set SLICE_MAX_UPPER_LIMIT\u003d4 and not have any issues, but I\u0027m not recommending that :-)\n\nAll this being said, if the goal is to have a better story around the entropy of the bits coming from TRNG_READ_DATA reads, the changes made will certainly not hurt that cause. I just wanted to point out what is happening and make sure everyone is ok with the throughput reduction.",
      "parentUuid": "f89c0978_e57ca3a7",
